2026-05-05 17:49:00.180 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls provide strong detection and prevention capabilities for unauthorized network traffic in ICS environments through multiple mechanisms. The control directly addresses the technique by: (1) implementing stateless and stateful inspection that can identify unauthorized traffic patterns based on source/destination/port/protocol mismatches against policy, (2) enabling protocol-aware filtering that recognizes anomalous ICS protocol usage patterns, (3) supporting allowlist-based access control that inherently blocks any traffic not explicitly permitted, and (4) facilitating network segmentation that restricts lateral movement and unauthorized command/control communications. The control is particularly effective at detecting automated exploitation and less sophisticated attackers who do not attempt to obfuscate traffic as legitimate protocol exchanges. However, the rating does not reach 5 because: (1) firewalls may struggle to detect unauthorized traffic that masquerades as legitimate protocol behavior or uses encrypted channels with valid destination endpoints, (2) highly skilled adversaries can craft traffic that conforms to expected patterns (timing, volume, protocol compliance) while still accomplishing unauthorized objectives, (3) zero-day protocols or novel attack vectors may not trigger firewall rules if they use previously unknown communication patterns, and (4) firewalls typically cannot detect all forms of data exfiltration if the attacker uses allowed protocols and destinations. The control achieves approximately 75-85% coverage across typical OT environments where standard ICS protocols and network topology are well-understood and properly configured."}
