2026-05-05 17:52:00.262 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls have limited direct detection capability for impersonation attacks (T0856). While firewalls can enforce network access controls and filter traffic based on source/destination addresses, ports, and protocols, they cannot reliably detect when an attacker is impersonating legitimate network traffic or devices within allowed communication flows. Once traffic passes through firewall rules (either via allowlist or denylist mechanisms), the firewall has no means to verify the authenticity of the source or validate whether the device claiming to be a legitimate entity actually is. Protocol-aware and application-aware firewalls may detect some anomalous traffic patterns, but they cannot authenticate device identity or cryptographically verify legitimacy. Firewalls are architecturally relevant as they control what traffic reaches potential targets, but they offer no reliable detection mechanism for impersonation itself. An attacker impersonating a legitimate device would likely generate traffic that appears identical to legitimate traffic if they spoof addresses correctly, and the firewall would pass it through. Detection of impersonation typically requires authentication mechanisms (certificates, cryptographic validation, device fingerprinting) rather than network traffic filtering. Therefore, this control provides defense-in-depth by controlling access, but does not detect the impersonation technique itself."}
