2026-05-05 22:29:30.877 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls provide moderate detection coverage for Remote Services (T0886) abuse in ICS environments. The control can effectively detect and prevent lateral movement using remote services through several mechanisms: (1) access control policies that restrict which assets can initiate remote service connections, (2) protocol-aware filtering that can identify and block unauthorized remote service traffic patterns, (3) network segmentation that limits which systems can reach external-facing remote services, and (4) stateful inspection that can detect anomalous connection attempts. However, coverage is limited to 50-75% of vectors because: firewalls primarily focus on network-level blocking rather than detecting legitimate remote service usage by compromised accounts, they cannot distinguish between authorized and unauthorized use of legitimately open remote service ports (SSH, RDP, etc.) without additional context, encrypted remote service traffic (VPN, SSH) is difficult to inspect at the protocol level, and sophisticated attackers can tunnel remote service traffic through allowed protocols or use legitimately provisioned remote access mechanisms. The control is most effective against unskilled attackers using unauthorized remote services or making obvious lateral movement attempts, but will struggle with attackers using stolen credentials to access properly configured remote services or employing traffic obfuscation. Network-based firewalls are architecturally aligned with preventing remote service abuse through access controls and segmentation, but their detection capability depends heavily on whether remote service access is already restricted by policy."}
