2026-05-05 22:29:53.887 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls have minimal direct functional alignment with detecting spearphishing link attacks. While firewalls can theoretically block traffic to known malicious domains through URL filtering or denylists, this approach has significant limitations: (1) Spearphishing emails typically arrive through legitimate email channels that firewalls are not designed to inspect or control at the content level; (2) Firewalls operate at network layers 3-4 and some application layers, but email link inspection requires email gateway-level controls, not network firewalls; (3) Firewalls cannot reliably distinguish between legitimate and malicious links within email bodies without deep packet inspection and threat intelligence integration, which is not a primary firewall function; (4) Attackers commonly use newly registered or legitimate-appearing domains that would not be on existing denylists; (5) Even if a firewall blocks a malicious destination, the initial spearphishing email delivery and user click still occur, so detection of the technique itself does not happen. The control would only detect this technique if an attacker makes a significant error, such as using a previously identified malicious domain or if the organization maintains an exceptionally comprehensive URL blocklist. Network-based firewalls are architecturally irrelevant to email-based phishing attacks and do not address the core mechanism of spearphishing—deceiving users into clicking malicious links within email messages."}
