2026-05-05 22:30:07.599 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls have limited direct detection capability for T0866 (Exploitation). While firewalls can provide some defense-in-depth by restricting network access and implementing segmentation, they do not inherently detect exploitation attempts themselves. A stateless or stateful firewall typically operates at network/transport layers (Layers 3-4) and filters based on IP addresses, ports, and protocols—it cannot analyze payload content or detect application-level exploitation attacks unless configured with advanced inspection capabilities. Even protocol-aware firewalls for ICS protocols and NGFWs with intrusion detection features would struggle to reliably detect exploitation of unpatched software because: (1) exploitation techniques vary widely and are often zero-day or variant-based, making signature-based detection unreliable; (2) many exploits can be transmitted over allowed traffic channels; (3) firewalls do not monitor the actual execution or impact of code on target systems. While a firewall may block some malicious traffic based on known threat signatures or suspicious patterns, it would only detect exploitation if the attacker makes significant errors (e.g., using well-known exploit traffic patterns) or if specific exploit signatures are pre-configured. In a typical OT environment with defense-in-depth segmentation, a firewall might prevent some lateral movement post-exploitation, but this is not detection of the exploitation technique itself. The control is architecturally relevant to restricting attack surface but offers no reliable detection of exploitation attempts."}
