2026-05-31 18:21:18.769 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"A SIEM system is architecturally well-suited to detect message blocking attacks (T0804) through analysis of reporting message patterns and flow, but faces practical limitations in comprehensive coverage. Here's the detailed assessment:\n\nStrengths:\n1. SIEM can detect anomalous patterns in telemetry reporting - sudden absence of expected I/O values, sensor readings, or status updates that should occur at regular intervals can be flagged as abnormal.\n2. If properly configured with baseline behavior analytics, SIEM can establish expected reporting frequencies and alert when messages from specific devices/systems cease or drop significantly.\n3. SIEM correlation rules can identify when expected status updates fail to arrive while other system activity continues, indicating selective message blocking.\n4. SIEM can correlate multiple data sources (network traffic logs, application logs, protocol analyzers) to detect when reporting messages are being dropped at network or device levels.\n5. In environments with comprehensive logging, SIEM can track message flow end-to-end and identify where blockage occurs.\n\nLimitations:\n1. SIEM detection depends heavily on quality baseline data - without understanding normal reporting patterns, anomalies are harder to identify. Many ICS environments lack mature baseline establishment.\n2. Gradual or selective message blocking (e.g., blocking only certain parameters while allowing others) may not trigger alerts if thresholds aren't finely tuned.\n3. SIEM primarily detects at the logging level - if reporting message blocking occurs at the protocol or physical layer without corresponding log entries, detection becomes difficult.\n4. Requires operators to have detection rules specifically designed for message blocking; generic SIEM configurations may miss this technique.\n5. SIEM is passive and relies on other systems generating appropriate logs; if blocking occurs in systems with minimal logging, SIEM cannot compensate.\n\nCoverage Assessment:\nA SIEM with well-tuned anomaly detection rules and comprehensive data sources can reliably detect message blocking in 50-75% of scenarios (e.g., obvious cessation of device reporting, unexplained gaps in telemetry streams). However, it will miss sophisticated attacks where blocking is partial, gradual, or occurs in poorly logged systems. This aligns with a rating of 3 - sufficient for moderate coverage and detection of moderately skilled attackers."}
