2026-05-31 18:21:28.474 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"SIEM systems provide moderate detection capability for DoS attacks through event aggregation and correlation, but with significant limitations in ICS environments. Strengths include: (1) ability to detect volumetric DoS attacks by correlating multiple failed connection attempts, error messages, or resource exhaustion indicators from network sensors and device logs; (2) detection of suspicious request patterns that don't conform to expected protocols; (3) correlation of device restart/shutdown events with preceding suspicious activity; (4) identification of reconnaissance activities (ping sweeps, probes) that precede DoS attacks. However, SIEM detection faces substantial challenges: (1) ICS DoS attacks can be extremely simple (e.g., a single malformed packet or ping sweep that immediately crashes legacy devices), making them difficult to distinguish from legitimate anomalies without extensive tuning; (2) detection relies on comprehensive log collection and proper sensor placement—many ICS environments lack adequate instrumentation, making attack visibility incomplete; (3) many DoS effects (device unresponsiveness, state disruption) are symptoms rather than detectable attack activities themselves; (4) exploitation of vulnerabilities to cause DoS may leave minimal forensic evidence in logs; (5) PDoS attacks may cause devices to fail in ways that prevent logging of the attack itself. SIEM can reliably detect approximately 50-75% of volumetric and reconnaissance-based DoS vectors in well-instrumented environments, but struggles with single-packet exploits and vulnerability-based DoS methods common in ICS. The tool is more effective against moderately skilled attackers using standard DoS techniques than against sophisticated attackers targeting specific device vulnerabilities."}
